the docs state that the lac sensor has an offset; for this track (140731), computing manually:
    derived from datpoint 359 / 4.7754 sec elapsed distances by a = 2d/t^2, the lacs reported when should have been zero were: [0.00596, -0.0937, -0.0354]
using the raw lac values, the final computed position was [0,2407, -3.0059, -1.5535]
using the above offsets, the position at 359 was close to zeroed out: [-3.775e-05, -0.0003246, -0.005290]
and the final position was: [-0.006695, 0.88395, -0.0839]
programmatically analyzing the lac values through 359 gives:
    mean: [0.004589053599944445, -0.08244096507222222, -0.03665653591666666]
    sigma: [0.008955879095290826, 0.025605613131009567, 0.0354847935848948]
statistical significance, though I'm not sure how much it means:
    > pnorm(2)
    [1] 0.9772499
    > 2 * (1 - pnorm(2))
    [1] 0.04550026
    > 2 * (1 - pnorm(4589/8955))
    [1] 0.6083353
    > 2 * (1 - pnorm(8244/2560))
    [1] 0.001280509
    > 2 * (1 - pnorm(3665/3548))
    [1] 0.301615
using the calculated means as offsets gives position at 359 as [0.01559, -0.1287, 0.0090368] and final position as [0.0502, 0.4165, -0.03182]; also gives final y-velocity of 0.2117; the velocities at 359 are all sub-millimeter
so looks like (as I should have anticipated) this is going to be unusable for centimeter-level precision

