looking at my 20260706-202528 ride, acceleration during the sprint phase was around 80-160 mm/s^2, and during the more regular cruise phase it ranged from occasionally
as low as 30 mm/s^2 to more typically ~120 mm/s^2 to at one point as high as 500 mm/s^2
so I think this is looking promising. the noise in the perturbation to the existing trajectory (1/2)a(dt)^2 will be (1/2)(6 mm/s^2)(0.1s)^2 = 0.03 mm over a period
that plausibly covers 50-100 cm. the noise in the perturbation to the velocity a(dt) will be (6 mm/s^2)(0.1 s) = 0.6 mm/s which over 5 seconds = 50 periods compounds to
at maximum 30 mm/s, which would create ~75 mm of error in the final position - over a period that plausibly covers 25-50 m. but this seems to be noise i.e. averaging
out to zero (it ran for 61 s without the reading seeming to drift), so really it looks like the performance will be even sigificantly better. this is almost suspiciously
good, though; we'll have to see what happens when it's under real and/or variable acceleration.

