i think I've figured out that, given some rotation, the rotation angle around a given axis is the angle between the starting
and ending vector when projected into the plane perpendicular to the axis. for example, suppose we rotate from (12, 5, 0) to (12, 3, 4).
for rotation about the x-axis: projection into yz-plane is (5, 0) to (3, 4), so cos theta = 15 / 25
y-axis: projection into zx-plane is (0, 12) to (4, 12), so cos theta = 144 / (12* sqrt(160))
z-axis: projection into xy-plane is (12, 5) to (12, 3), so cos theta = 159 / (13 * sqrt(153))

it appears that I am wrong:
    [nibawr@amaranth ~]$ cat test
    #!/usr/bin/env python

    from math import acos, sqrt, asin
    import numpy as np

    rot_x = acos(15 / 25)
    rot_y = -acos(144 / (12 * sqrt(160)))
    rot_z = -acos(159 / (13 * sqrt(153)))
    print(rot_x, rot_y, rot_z)

    omega = sqrt(rot_x**2 + rot_y**2 + rot_z**2);
    print(omega)

    rot_axis = [theta / omega for theta in [rot_x, rot_y, rot_z]]
    print(rot_axis)

    v1 = np.array([12, 5, 0])
    v2 = np.array([12, 3, 4])
    cross = np.cross(v1, v2)
    print(cross)

    cross_norm = np.linalg.norm(cross)
    computed_theta = asin(cross_norm / 169)
    computed_theta_2 = acos(np.dot(v1, v2) / 169)
    computed_rot_axis = cross / cross_norm
    print(computed_theta)
    print(computed_theta_2)
    print(computed_rot_axis)
    [nibawr@amaranth ~]$ ./test
